A light pole collapsed on the road, causing serious injuries to people in Rolea Bier district, Kampong Chhnang province
(Kampong Chhnang): Due to a rotten light pole, it fell to the ground, causing the power line to fall across the road, causing a man on a motorcycle to be hit by a pipe and seriously injured. This incident happened on January 12, 2021 in Teuk Chech village, Cheung Kreav commune, Rolea Bier district, Kampong Chhnang province.
After the incident, the victim's friend, who was the driver of the motorcycle, was immediately taken to a private doctor in the village for treatment.
The victim stated that the above case was caused by the electricity users in the village planting small bamboo poles, and some of the poles were rotten, causing the poles to break and fall on the road. Victims, as well as residents and local authorities, appealed to consumers to use strong poles to ensure the safety of all of the above when connecting wires to the house.
Local authorities have stated that in the above case, if the victim complains, the lamp maker will be responsible before the law. However, the victim stated that he did not want to go far, just asked people to think about the safety of others when using electricity.
Phnom Penh Capital Administration Plans to Rehabilitate Main Canal in Dangkor, Kambol and Po Sen Chey Districts to Relieve Rainy Season Floods
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Khuong Sreng, Governor of Phnom Penh, confirmed that the Phnom Penh Capital Administration has put in place a plan to rehabilitate drainage canals in three districts: Dangkor, Kampol and Por Sen Chey to relieve the flood. Faster from the local population in the coming rainy season.
Mr. Khuong Sreng made this remark during a meeting with his subordinates on the afternoon of January 14, 2021 to restore one of the main canals, the Toul Pong 1 canal, as the area is often flooded. Rain in the past.
During the meeting, Mr. Khuong Sreng urged the relevant departments such as Department of Water Resources, Department of Public Works and Transport and Khan authorities to rehabilitate the Tuol Pongro 1 canal with a length of more than 4,000 meters. In order to invite the people involved in this canal to come to a meeting to solve this problem, we must speed up this work in a timely manner.
However, there is no good solution in this meeting yet, as the Phnom Penh Capital Administration will hold a meeting next week to implement the work of restoring the canal successfully to avoid flooding of people's houses. .
Cassava is the second strategic agricultural crop after rice, Cambodia starts promoting cassava cultivation and processing
(Phnom Penh): The Minister of Commerce, Mr. Pan Sosak, highlighted that cassava is an agro-industrial crop and an important export product of Cambodia, which contributes 3% to 4% of GDP. Cassava is also the second largest agricultural crop after rice and has also contributed significantly to national economic development.
Minister Pan Sosak emphasized this during the official dissemination ceremony of the National Policy on Cassava 2020-2025, which was held on January 14, 2021 at the Ministry of Commerce. The event was also attended by representatives of ministries, institutions in the public sector, the private sector and various development partners working in this field in Cambodia.
According to Mr. Pan Sosak, the National Policy on Cassava 2020-2025 was approved by the Royal Government of Cambodia on August 14, 2020. This national policy aims to make Cambodia a location for production, processing and a reliable supplier of cassava products to boost exports to the world market.
This policy also has three main goals: first: to change from traditional or family cassava production to commercial cassava production, second: to support potential cassava processors and attract investment to process products from High value-added cassava to supply the market; and third, to enhance trade competitiveness by penetrating and capturing market share by linking the relevant sectors in exports.
Cassava is an agro-commercial crop for export and not a staple food for Cambodians, but it is the most important crop after rice, corn and wheat. Cassava is grown by farmers in rural areas and it has become a potential crop due to the rapidly growing area and quantity of products.
Mr. Pan Sosak said that each year, the cassava sector receives an investment of about 300 million US dollars, with more than 60,000 hectares of cultivated land. In 2019, Cambodia exported about 3.30 million tons of dried cassava and flour worth more than 26.40 million US dollars.
The Minister of Commerce drew attention to the participants in the cassava policy promotion program that cassava is the first and foremost business, thus requiring more private sector investment, both domestic and foreign, in the value chain. Production, processing and marketing.
"In order for the implementation of the above national policy to be successful, it is necessary to have the cooperation and support of all stakeholders, including the public, private and development partners," he said.
Mr. Pan Sosak also said that in order to tap the potential of the cassava sector, Cambodia should improve its value chain by strengthening existing capacity and creating new capacity to capture emerging market opportunities for overall economic growth. And expand its economy to participate in achieving the goals of the Royal Government of Cambodia 2030 and 2050.
The Minister of Commerce expressed strong hope that this national policy is the beginning of a new journey for Cambodia to develop the agribusiness sector, which will bring more economic growth to Cambodia in the context. Low middle income countries and support the Royal Government's plan towards high middle income countries in the future.
In order to effectively implement the National Policy on Cassava 2020-2025, Mr. Pan Sosak decided to establish a Technical Working Group as a Secretariat with the participation of relevant ministries, institutions and the private sector to monitor and evaluate the effectiveness of the implementation of this national policy. By sustainability and responsibility.
This technical working group is responsible for monitoring and evaluating proposals to improve implementation and reporting to the Royal Government through the Economic and Financial Policy Committee. Ministry of Tourism Announces Training Course for National Trainers and National Chef Assessors in ASEAN Standards
