(Battambang): Residents living along the banks of the Sangke River behind the Victory Club have expressed dissatisfaction with the Battambang provincial authorities for allowing them to demolish their houses from the river, which they claim to have built and lived in since 1990.
Ms. Pen Sokunthea, representing the people who were required by the authorities to demolish their houses from the river, told Fresh News by phone on January 13, 2021 that her family and about 19 families who were relocated The house on the banks of the Sangker River after the West Coast Victory Club for 30 years has been ordered to be demolished by the authorities.
Ms. Pen Sokunthea, whose husband is also a civil servant, described that the authorities led by Mr. Soeum Bunrith, Deputy Governor of Battambang Province, came down to advise them to dismantle their houses before 2021. She said that the authorities asked them to rent a house temporarily, waiting for the authorities to build a house on a land size of 4 and a half meters by 8 meters as compensation.
Ms. Pen Sokunthea expressed dissatisfaction with the authorities' solution. She said that the compensation for the house that the authorities exchanged was smaller than her current house, not enough for her family with many members.
She added that allowing her family and other families to rent a house temporarily without knowing when to buy a house is a challenge in life at a time when people are losing income because. The Kovid 19 crisis.
Ms. Pen Sokunthea stated that "the threat to dismantle and compensate the land is small and very urgent, so we can not accept because there are many family members. "Such exchanges seem to be forced, unresolved, as in other provinces, without prior notice, and now the children are unemployed and losing income due to Kovid 19."
Ms. Pen Sokunthea also appealed to Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng, Deputy Prime Minister, Minister of Interior and Chairman of the Battambang Provincial Assistance Team to help find a suitable solution for her and other people.
However, according to sources obtained by Fresh News, the relocation of houses from the mouth of the Sangke River is to improve the garden to enhance the beauty in the city.
