Article 7 of the Royal Decree stipulates that a convict can receive a commutation of his sentence only if he has served one-third of the sentence imposed by the court. For life imprisonment, the convict must first be sentenced to 10 years.
1: Imprisonment from 1 year to less than 5 years may reduce the imprisonment period to 3 months.
2. Imprisonment for 5 years to less than 10 years may be reduced to 6 months or 3 months.
Inmates sentenced to life in prison after being reduced to 30 years could receive multiple commutations. However, even though the prisoner has been pardoned several times, the total sentence must be at least 20 years.
