Two workers on a poultry farm infected with bird flu were taken to the Institut Pasteur for inspection.
(Battambang): Two workers caring for chickens at a chicken farm suspected of having the H5N1 bird flu outbreak were taken to the Baster Institute in Phnom Penh for questioning, and officials put them in one place. Not allowed to go out to meet others.
According to the report on January 13, 2021, Mr. Voeung Bun Reth, Director of Battambang Provincial Health Department, yesterday visited two workers who were caring for chickens in a farm where there was an outbreak of H5N1 bird flu. In Prek Kanchreng village, Prek Luong commune, Ek Phnom district, Battambang province, sent to Pasteur Institute, Phnom Penh. The two workers who took care of and touched the dead chickens were Ron Panha, 21, and Khon Chivoan, 28.
Dr. Ouk Vitya, Deputy Director of the Battambang Provincial Health Department, said that the health of the two workers was normal and there were no significant symptoms.
According to Ho Nam, the owner of the farm, the farm has been raising chickens for a month, importing 1,300 breeds from a Vietnamese company and 200 farm chickens, more than 200 ducks and geese. Some.
According to the care workers, it was confirmed that the chickens started to get sick and died from January 31 to 5, all of them died at once, and these dead chickens were instructed to be buried and sprayed with disinfectant. Around the farm as well.
It should be noted that Mr. Veng Sakhon, Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, confirmed on January 11 that the test results on more than 1,000 dead chickens in Prek Ta Chreng village, Prek Narin commune, Ek Phnom district, Battambang province showed that: Avian Influenza (H5N1), but this type of disease does not affect humans.
Mr. Chhim Vachira, Director of the Battambang Provincial Department of Agriculture, appealed to residents that if they see sick or dead chickens and suspected bird flu, they must immediately report to the local authorities and officials of the veterinary office or the Department of Agriculture to intervene immediately.
