(Phnom Penh): Senior Minister Sun Chanthol, Minister of Public Works and Transport, expressed his desire to see the next generation of Cambodian children able to build bridges across the river, leaving behind national pride like the Cambodian ancestors who built temples and bridges left over for thousands of years.
To achieve this goal, Senior Minister Sun Chanthol, under the guidance of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, decided to establish the Techo Sen Institute of Public Works and Transport. The institute will train technical skills in the field of public works and transportation, especially the construction of roads and bridges.
Senior Minister Sun Chanthol recalled the many works that Khmer ancestors built during the Angkorian period, such as temples and bridges that are thousands of years old in Siem Reap. But why can't Cambodians today build bridges across the Mekong, Bassac, and Tonle Sap rivers?
With an expression of excitement at the historical achievements of the Institute of Public Works and Transport Skills Training, Senior Minister Sun Chanthol said, "Today we have opened a new page in history under the wise leadership of Samdech Akka Moha Sena Padei Techo Hun. Sen in training our resources to participate in building our country more prosperous.
Mr. Sun Chanthol also said that currently, due to the lack of technical experts, every time a road or bridge is built, foreigners are often hired to build it. Therefore, this institute will contribute to training Cambodian children in building roads and bridges without relying on foreign experts.
"There is nothing to rent to foreigners until there is money, but only to hire them to build roads and bridges. Therefore, I believe that the graduates of Techo Sen Institute of Public Works and Transport, in collaboration with the Institute of Technology of Cambodia, will have sufficient capacity to build good quality roads and bridges in Cambodia. The Minister of Public Works and Transport expressed hope.
The Senior Minister expressed his expectation and belief that after the presence of Techo Sen Institute of Public Works and Transport, Khmer children will be able to build roads and bridges across the river without relying on foreign technicians. Philippine president orders crackdown on internet service providers that fail to curb child pornography
