"Art of Sharing" Painting Exhibition will contribute to 3 organizations in Cambodia by raising funds from philanthropists
(Phnom Penh): The "Art of Sharing" Painting Exhibition is organized by the Museum of Economy and Currency of Preah Isanvarman and under the initiative of Ms. Chea Serey, Assistant Governor of the National Bank of Cambodia and an artist. Raised to raise funds for three organizations in Cambodia working on social and mental health issues.
The "Art of Sharing" exhibition was first held on January 10, 2021 and is open to the public for free. This is also the first time that the exhibition in Cambodia has been organized as a 3D digital exhibition that the public can access freely through the internet and everywhere.
Ms. Chea Serey said that in these difficult times, we need to unite with each other, creating hope and happiness for each other, such as art exhibitions to raise funds for this charity.
Chea Serey's initiative on the painting emerged during the re-emergence of Kovid 19, requiring everyone to stay at home. During her break, she took time to paint, during which time she recalled her intention to use her paintings for the benefit of society.
In order to contribute to the Cambodian society, Ms. Adana Mamlegros, a French artist of Khmer descent and a talent for painting, also contributed to the joint exhibition for this charity.
According to Adana Mamlegros, the main motivation for participating in this activity was her parents' advocacy activities and her personal experience with cancer. On the other hand, she has been interested in this humanitarian work since she was young, so she really enjoys presenting her work in this joint exhibition for charity.
According to the spokesperson, within 10 days of tightening the road traffic law by increasing the fine according to Sub-Decree No. 39, from May 1 to January 10, 2021, the traffic police across the country inspected and A total of 8,986 vehicles were fined, including 6,672 motorcycles and 2,314 vehicles of all types.
Notable offenses for motorcycles are mostly helmet offense, sign offense, no license plate overtaking, cell phone overtaking and drunkenness. The offenses for cars are mostly speeding, speeding, drunken driving, overtaking, driving without a seat belt and using a mobile phone.
