CPP Members in Santuk District Celebrate the 42nd Anniversary of January 7 Victory Day
(Kampong Thom): Members of the Cambodian People's Party (CPP) in Santuk district, Kampong Thom province, gathered to celebrate the "42nd Anniversary of January 7 Victory Day, the day Cambodia was rescued and liberated from Democratic Kampuchea."
The ceremony "Celebrating the 42nd Anniversary of the Victory Day, January 7 (January 7, 1979 - January 7, 2021), was held with the participation of Mr. Chea Chanto, Permanent Member of the Central Committee of the Cambodian People's Party and Chairman of the National Working Group for Stok District.
In a message from Samdech Techo Hun Sen, President of the Cambodian People's Party, read to the ceremony by Mr. Chea Chanto, it is clear that after the country was liberated from the brutal regime on January 7, 1979 under the leadership From the party to the Cambodian people, our people have full rights and freedoms, and the whole country is peaceful and developed in all areas, including Cambodia has a prestige and a new face on the international stage.
The message also reminded that 42 years ago, all Cambodians, including young children, were forced to work overtime, have no days off, do not have enough food, no hospital, no school, and all citizens All are living without rights and freedoms.
With this message, we call for the Victory Day on January 7 and the spirit of National Liberation Day to be in the hearts of all citizens and as a symbol of national unity and the great Khmer family to be immortal under the shadow of eternal peace.
(Kandal): The National Eye Health Program of the Ministry of Health has provided materials to prevent the spread of Kovid-19 to the Kandal Provincial Health Department for the second time for use in all referral hospitals and health centers throughout Kandal Province.
These items, organized by the National Eye Health Program under the auspices of the Australian Government through the Free Hollow Foundation, include: 101,400 masses, 3,840 liters of alcohol, 131 thermometers, 1,200 hand sanitizers, condoms 27,200 arms and 1,000 face masks.
In addition, the National Eye Health Program also provided additional equipment to hospitals and other provincial health departments after providing Kovid-19 combat equipment to referral hospitals and health centers throughout Kandal province on January 6. 2021.
It should be noted that with the support of the Australian Government through the Fred Holo Foundation, the National Eye Health Program has already provided equipment to prevent the spread of Kovid-19 disease to hospitals in all capitals and provinces once in mid-2020.
Professor Ngy Meng, Director of the National Ophthalmology Program and Director of the Khmer-Soviet Friendship Hospital, said that these materials have been contributing significantly to helping the Royal Government to prevent the spread of Kovid-19 disease in Cambodia, especially Facilitate doctors to provide eye health services to people with confidence.
Professor Ngy Meng also encouraged people with vision problems to get eye examination and treatment at the nearest health center or hospital in a timely manner.
According to the director of the National Eye Health Program, hospitals and health centers across Kandal province will provide eye examination and treatment services to tens of thousands of people by 2020, despite the world facing Kovid-19.
On behalf of the National Eye Health Program of the Ministry of Health and the people, he thanked the Australian Government and the Fred Holo Foundation for their continued support of the eye health sector in Cambodia. Crackdown on drug distribution mastermind from Phnom Penh to Poipet by arresting 2 suspects and a large number of exhibits
