(Phnom Penh): Mr. Has Vary, Personal Advisor on behalf of Samdech Techo and Samdech Kittiprittbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen, together with his colleagues, inspected the construction process of fences and schools in Roka Kong I and II communes, Muk Kampoul district, Kandal province.
In addition to the inspection, there was also a ceremony to open the eyes of the Buddha statue to wish the people and leaders of the country to be free from evil and especially to stay away from Kovid 19 and to celebrate. Congratulations on the 42nd anniversary of the January 7, 2021 Victory Day.
So far, Mr. Has Vary has spent 55 million riels to buy a 120-acre plot of land in Spean Thmey village at the request of the people of Roka Kong 1 and 2 communes to be used as a cemetery for the people living in this area. Until now, he has also built a fence and a school in the area as a shrine to the local people.
As always, Samdech Techo Hun Sen has assigned Mr. Has Vary to help solve all the shortcomings of the people in Roka Kong I and II communes on a regular basis.
At the same time, on January 7, 2021, the 42nd anniversary of the January 7 Victory Day, which the Royal Government of Cambodia always celebrates to thank the cadres, warriors and warriors who sacrificed their lives to exchange fresh flesh and blood. Peace and freedom for the motherland.
Victory Day, January 7, is the day that Cambodians across the country were freed from the clutches of the deadly regime of Democratic Kampuchea, the regime led by the most brutal genocidal group Pol Pot, under the life-changing struggle of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, along with patriotic dignitaries. More.
If we go back to the regime of 3 years, 8 months and 20 days, it is a horrible memory for the Cambodian people who went through this regime and were lucky enough to survive to this day. Mr. Mao Buntha is now 62 years old, a Cambodian citizen who suffered the same torture and tragedy as other Cambodians because of the Khmer Rouge regime.
Before the Khmer Rouge took over the country, he was a scholar whose parents were farmers living in the Chroy Changva area of Phnom Penh. He was about 17 years old at the time.
When the Khmer Rouge attacked Phnom Penh, Buntha and more than a dozen families were evacuated to Battambang province. There, in the name of the New People, or Khmer Rouge, also known as the "April 17s," his family was torn apart and he was assigned to the Khmer Rouge by the Khmer Rouge.
One day after arriving in the new land, Buntha and other young men from Phnom Penh were summoned by the Khmer Rouge to inquire about their work and occupation in Phnom Penh.
Living in the mobile unit, Mr. Buntha, who used to carry books and pens, was assigned by the Khmer Rouge to do a lot of work, such as plowing, plowing, harvesting, and many other jobs as ordered by Khmer Rouge cadres. Living in the status of the Khmer Rouge on April 17, his diet was divided into months, namely, months to eat, months to eat plain porridge, and months to eat liquid porridge with a can of rice to eat together. There are also 10 people.
In a group of young people who had to work hard and did not eat enough until they were about to die, Buntha and 17 other degenerate youths were abandoned by the Khmer Rouge on a hill waiting to die.
When he died, he was abandoned to wait for the day of his death, but after he tried to feed himself, he was caught by the Khmer Rouge fishing in the Tonle Sap to provide food for the cadres. Back. After returning, this is when he saw his father die of starvation in front of him.
Despite fleeing from one mobile unit to another to escape forced marriage, he was still unable to escape the deadly prison-like regime. After being captured by the Khmer Rouge, he was tortured, handcuffed, and starved to death, becoming a rotting wound and worm-infested. However, he was able to survive after being rescued by the Front Army.
As a survivor of four regimes, Mao Buntha urged the younger generation to keep the peace, because without peace the nation could not develop.
