Svay Rieng Municipality Inspects and Visits Farmers Growing Vegetables, Fish and Dry Rice
(Svay Rieng): Mr. Nop Chamroeun, Governor of Svay Rieng City, this morning, January 6, 2021, led a working group to inspect and visit people who grow vegetables, fish and dry rice in Svay Rieng City.
Svay Rieng City Hall officials said that this morning, the mayor, together with relevant officials, inspected the dry rice production of Mr. Chhem Sopheap, a dry rice farmer in Koy Trabek village, Koy Trabek commune.
Mr. Chhem Sopheap confirmed that this is the first time that he has leased 20 hectares of land in Koy Trabek village, Koy Trabek commune for dry rice and plans to grow more cucumbers.
In addition, the team continued to visit the people who grow beetroot in Svay village, Sangkat Chek of Ms. Mok Samon 4 hectares, she confirmed that the beetroot is for pickling for sale in the market in Svay Rieng In addition to the seasons, she also grows other crops such as cucumbers, beans, pumpkins, gourds, etc.
On that occasion, Mr. Nop Chamroeun expressed his gratitude for the efforts of these businesses and encouraged the cultivation of all kinds of crops and animal husbandry to supply the local market to increase family income, contribute to Reduce poverty and improve the living standards of the people to a certain level.
In addition, the mayor instructed the director of the Office of Economy and Community Development to find partners to supply the market and support them to provide more technology.
He instructed all people to respect the traffic law and cooperate to prevent the spread of Kovid-19 in accordance with the instructions of the Ministry of Health. On that occasion, the mayor also provided 5 boxes of protective masks to the three families. South Korea launches large-scale Kovid-19 test on 70,000 prisoners and prison staff
SEOUL (Reuters) - South Korea has begun large-scale testing of the coronavirus (COVID-19) for 52 prisons across the country following a spate of cluster infections in prisons. It will also be able to delay flights from the UK in order to prevent the spread of the virus. This is according to the release by CNA on Wednesday, January 6, 2021.
South Korean authorities will also decide whether to suspend flights from the UK, as 12 new cases of COVID-19 infection have recently been discovered. South Korea has already imposed restrictions on direct flights from the United Kingdom until January 7, 2021, which requires passengers from Britain or South Africa to pass a pre-test. Before boarding the plane.
