China to allow foreign scientists to use giant telescope from April 1, 2021
BEIJING: China's FAST telescope, the world's largest, will be open to international scientists from April 1, after nearly a year of official operation. This was released by the Xinhua News Agency on Monday, January 4, 2021.
The National Astronomical Observatory (NAOC) of the Chinese Academy of Sciences, which operates the telescope, confirmed on Monday that from April 1, 2021, all scientists around the world will be able to start applying online. To the use of FAST telescopes for astronomical observations.
During the first year of opening the FAST telescope to the international scientific community, 10 percent of the operating time will be allocated to foreign scientists. The FAST telescope, located in southwest China's Guizhou Province, officially began operations on January 11, 2020.
Hua Chunying made the remarks at a briefing on Monday. She added that China is continuing to actively support local enterprises to facilitate the establishment of relations with foreign partners in research and development, as well as vaccine production.
