On the occasion of inspecting the construction of National Road No. 3 on January 4, 2021, Senior Minister Sun Chanthol, Minister of Public Works and Transport, said that Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Cambodia, has issued an order to build a flyover. Both pedestrians to prevent accidents at this busy crossing.
In the project to expand and rehabilitate National Road 3 from Chom Chao roundabout to Kampot city, the Ministry of Public Works has decided to build two bridges, one at Tram Khna Market and the other at Ang Ta Som Market and High School.
The project to build the pedestrian flyover has been blocked by the market traders, claiming that it will affect the beauty of the market and affect the access to the market.
An official source said that the pedestrian flyover, traders have sought the intervention of the province and the people to prevent the construction. According to the source, the obstruction of the traders is that they are afraid of affecting the entry and exit of the market.
Senior Minister Sun Chanthol emphasized that after such protests, he wrote a letter to Samdech Techo Hun Sen and Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng, describing the technical nature of the road construction.
The senior minister confirmed that National Road No. 3 is large, long and difficult to cross, which requires a pedestrian flyover to facilitate crossing and avoid traffic accidents.
"After the request of Samdech Techo Prime Minister, he decided to build because he was afraid of endangering the safety of the people when crossing this road," he said.
Please be informed that as of January 4, 2021, the construction of National Road No. 3 has been completed about 90%, and is scheduled to be completed in September 2021.
The expansion and upgrading of the road from DBST to AC on National Road No. 3 has a total length of nearly 135 km from the Chaom Chao roundabout to Kampot under a Chinese government concessional financing of more than 200 million US dollars.
Separately, the second section from Bek Kous to Kampot city, nearly 65 km long, was widened to 12 meters in two lanes (1 lane to 1 lane) with 7 cm thick AC rubber concrete, 11 meters wide.
