The agreement was reached last week after about seven years of negotiations. The deal could be a huge victory for Beijing, which is seeking new foreign investment as companies move out of China in the face of a Sino-US trade war.
The EU has said the deal, dubbed the "EU-China Comprehensive Investment Agreement" (CAI), would allow European companies to compete more fairly with state-owned companies in China, which is now a trading partner. The EU is the second largest country after the United States. The investment agreement still has to go through ratification by the European Parliament, which could take months.
According to some international analysts, the agreement threatens the possibility of an attempt to reconcile EU-US relations as the new US President Joe Biden takes office. In the coming weeks, the United States is showing its intention to rebuild a lasting alliance in Europe after it weakened under the Trump administration.
