WASHINGTON (Reuters) - The death toll in a passenger plane crash worldwide has risen to 299 by 2020, according to a Dutch-based consulting firm this weekend. Although the number of cases has dropped to more than 50 percent compared to the figure in 2019. This is according to the release by CNA on Saturday afternoon, January 2, 2021.
In 2020, there were 40 plane crashes involving large passenger planes, five of which killed 299 people, according to airline consulting firm To70. In 2019, there were a total of 86 air accidents, eight of which killed 257 people.
More than half of all deaths in the To70 review show that 176 people were killed in January 2020 when a Ukrainian plane was shot down over Iranian airspace. . In addition, the second most dangerous incident was the crash of a Pakistani passenger plane in May that killed 98 people.
It should be noted that large passenger planes cover almost all statistics, but do not include small passenger planes in the service. Over the last two decades, the death toll in the aviation industry has dropped dramatically. According to the Aviation Safety Network (ASN), in 2005 a total of 1,015 people died on commercial flights worldwide, a record high.
