General Knowledge: Which scholar was the first to discover the theory of universal gravity?
(Earth): In the past, people believed that the earth was the center of the universe and that the moon and the sun revolved around the earth, but later scientists interpreted it as the opposite. It is not the center of the universe, and the sun does not revolve around the earth, but the earth revolves around the sun, and seven other planets in the solar system revolve around the sun like the earth.
After the achievement of the development of this new telescope, in 1671, Newton became a professor of mathematics at the University of Cambridge, even though he was only 29 years old. The following year he was elected a member of the King James II Scientific Research Association of the United Kingdom.
After Newton came up with this hypothesis, he developed a special theorem, explaining mathematical formulas and handwriting on a piece of paper for himself, but he did not immediately publish the theory.
This theory of universal gravitation is the answer to the most important questions regarding the motion of the planets orbiting the sun and the motion of the moon orbiting the earth. European scholars and scientists at the time eventually came to understand the phenomenon of gravity and continued to spread it around the world.
