Indonesia plans to launch Sinovac vaccination campaign after receiving the first 1.2 million doses this December and expects to receive an additional 1.8 million doses in January 2021. Approved for emergency use by the Indonesian Medicines Regulator.
About 1.3 million front-line health workers will be vaccinated first in the first phase between January and April 2021. Civil servants, on the other hand, will be the next group of people, especially those living in infected areas with red markers between the ages of 18-59, who will be given priority in the second phase.
Indonesia on Monday announced that it would ban all international tourists from entering the country for two weeks in a bid to stem the spread of the new strain of coronavirus, which originated in the United Kingdom.
The new regulation will take effect on January 1, 2021, just days after the ASEAN nation announced a ban on travelers from the United Kingdom and tightened rules for travelers from Europe and the United States. Australia. Ms Marsudi stressed that the new rules would apply to all foreign tourists except senior government officials or foreigners who are allowed to stay in the country.
