General knowledge: Understand the formation of a NATO or NATO military organization
Taking European countries to follow the liberal influence of the United States and the Communists from the Soviet Union made them politically different throughout Europe, which dates back to World War II. 1939-1945). This confrontation provided a framework for military confrontation that continued until the end of the Cold War (1945-1991).
In response, the United States and its Western allies sought ways to curb the spread of communist influence throughout Europe. In 1947, US leaders launched the Marshall Plan, a diplomatic initiative to provide aid to the Allies to help them rebuild their economies and infrastructure destroyed by the war.
Events in the following years prompted US leaders to take a more militaristic stance toward the Soviet Union. In February 1949, a Soviet-backed coup overthrew the liberal government of Czechoslovakia and plunged the country into full communist camp. In the days that followed, US leaders joined forces to discuss a security treaty with their European allies.
This process led to the next incident in June of the same year, when the Socialist Republic of the Soviet Union split Germany in two. The severance between West Germany and East Germany forced the United States, Britain, and France to transport supplies by air to cities in West Germany. As a result, after World War II, Europe was divided.
Note: Did you know that? NATO continued its existence even after the end of the Cold War and gained new members in Eastern Europe during the early 1990s. This action did not have the support of the leaders of the Russian Federation, and it also became a source of tension between the East and the West after the end of the Cold War.
* NATO formation brings Western nations together
The first members of NATO were Belgium, the United Kingdom, Canada, Denmark, France, Ireland, Italy, Luxembourg, Nordland, Norway, Portugal and the United States. NATO has been working with Western military forces against the Soviet Union and its allies for about 40 years, and its membership has grown steadily due to the causes of the Cold War era.
Greece and Turkey were opened in 1952, the Federal Republic of Germany (West Germany) in 1955, and Spain in 1982. Dissatisfied with his role in NATO, France decided to withdraw its candidacy from the military in 1966, and that decision continued until 1995.
Countries that joined the Soviet Union included Albania, Bulgaria, Czechoslovakia, the Democratic Republic of Germany (East Germany), Hungary, Poland, and Romania. The group continued its existence until the Cold War ended with the overthrow of all communist governments in Eastern Europe in 1989 and 1990.
Like NATO, the Warsaw Pact focuses on the purpose of establishing a coordinated defense among its member states to prevent enemy attacks, in general, to counter American allies. The pact also contained internal security components that benefited the Soviet Union.
