Hong Kong activist Tony Chung was sentenced by a Hong Kong court to four months in prison on Tuesday for insulting a Chinese flag and illegally gathering in July. Increasingly on the prominent activists of this Asian financial center. This is according to the release by CNA on Tuesday night, December 29, 2020.
During his sentence, Chung is awaiting trial on charges of "inciting separatism" that could lead to life imprisonment under the new national security law. Beijing adopted on June 30, 2020.
The new security law has sparked criticism and accusations from Western countries that it would end a 50-year guarantee of freedom after Britain ended its rule in the country last year. 1997. The Chinese side called the new law a "sword" to restore order and stability to the financial center after facing protests for almost a year.
