On the evening of December 26, 2020, Ms. Sun Samnang, 41, of Ang Ta Set village, Tuol Prech commune, Ang Snuol district, Kandal province, claimed in front of the media that she had plantation land. A three-and-a-half-hectare plot of land with plot 582 and her brother with 12 plots of land was blocked due to road closures.
"When she first bought the land, there was a normal access road, so she decided to buy the land. In recent years, Khov Keat has been fencing off the road and locking the doors," she said. That leaves her with no access to their plantations.
She stated that during the period when there was no access to her plantation, all the crops in her plantation and fence posts, including a plantation hut, were burned to the ground, not to mention a three-dimensional tree. Her beloved embrace was also stolen from her land.
"The latest news is that more than 4 kilometers of the road has reached her 582 plot, which can not be connected to the other side of the road," she said. So, if the state really needs to open a road that is more than 200 meters long or more, she will donate land to the state for public road use voluntarily so that the general public can travel with her thumbprint. Give the land immediately and free of charge.
Currently, Mr. Ly Sophea, Director of the Provincial Cadastral Office is working to get Mr. Khov Keat to reopen this road in the near future.
