The Korean War marked the beginning of the United States' long-term blockade policy, which proved that the influence of communism did not spread to other parts of the world. After the Korean War, there was a lull in Southeast Asia, and a new military coalition, the Seattle, was formed.
Establishment of the Seattle Organization On February 19, 1955, at least eight countries formed the Seattle Organization. The eight member states are the United States, the United Kingdom, France, New Zealand, Australia, Pakistan, Thailand and the Philippines. The group's mission is very simple: to stop the spread of communism throughout Asia.
* What are the reasons for joining SETO? It should be noted that only two of the eight member countries of SATO are located in Southeast Asia, namely Thailand and the Philippines. So what are the reasons for other members to join Sierra Leone?
Australia and New Zealand are located in the Pacific, not far from Southeast Asia, so proximity is a big factor. Pakistan is also close to each other (in South Asia) and is joining the Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries in the hope of receiving assistance from Sierra Leone in resolving issues with India, even though Pakistan and India are not under Jurisdiction of the Seattle.
Britain and France had close ties with Southeast Asia, and both countries colonized the region before World War II. The main purpose of the United States in joining the organization was to reduce the influence of communism, especially from China.
In addition to the members of the organization, three other countries have been granted observer status. Although the three countries are not technically part of the organization, they are under the protection of the organization. Those countries include Laos, Cambodia and Vietnam.
Joining the Philippine and Thai seo organizations because of their close ties to the United States, especially the Philippines, and they faced communist uprisings from anti-government groups. Thailand became a member of the group when it discovered the newly created "Thai Autonomous Region" in southern China's Yunnan province, which was apparently under threat of Chinese communist subversion on its territory.
Several other states that had just gained independence from French Indochina (including North Vietnam, South Vietnam, Cambodia and Laos) were barred from joining the international military allies due to the Geneva Accords. Signed on July 20 (the year of the end of the First Indochina War).
However, with the continuing threat from communist North Vietnam and the possibility of domino theory with Indochina turning into a communist frontier, the SARS underwent these countries under its protection. It was considered an important solution for the United States in connection with the Vietnam War, but Cambodia rejected its defense in 1956.
* When did the Caucasus dissolve, and for what reason? The Caucasus also had weaknesses, although it was established with the main purpose of suppressing communist influence, but failed to establish itself due to internal strife and conflict. Use its military.
To resolve issues related to guerrilla movements and insurgencies in the region, Seattle has only called for consultation, leaving the troubled nations to resolve their own internal threats.
