Legal Awareness: Re-trial
(Phnom Penh): In principle, a verdict or civil judgment that has already been finalized, the parties have no right to appeal, but if the decision is flawed in the proceedings of the complaint or documents that are the basis of the verdict or The verdict has serious defects or irregularities, which is why this decision did not give justice to the other party.
In this case, what should the other party who suffered the injustice do to have another decision?
Therefore, the legal awareness program of FRESH NEWS in collaboration with the Cambodian Bar Association for International Cooperation would like to raise the topic of "Re-trial" to the following students:
In order to be transparent and fair to the retrial, the court that has the exclusive jurisdiction of the trial is the court that issued the final verdict or final judgment. However, the composition of the judges who have already participated in the verdict or final judgment on the substance of the matter may not be able to participate in the trial and adjudication of the claim for re-trial.
Appeals for a retrial shall be made within 30 days from the date on which the parties became aware of the reasons for the retrial after the verdict has been finalized, or five years from the date of the final judgment. From the final verdict or judgment or the reason for the retrial.
On the other hand, a request for a retrial may be made by a third party in the event that the plaintiff and the defendant conspire to accept a verdict or judgment for the purpose of depriving the third party of their rights and interests. In this case, the plaintiff and the defendant become co-defendants. For the procedure of re-trial, the procedure of the first trial shall follow the same procedure.
In summary, a retrial is a civil procedure that gives the parties the opportunity to find a cause of injustice by a final verdict or judgment that may require a retrial. However, this re-trial does not allow the parties to the case to demand the trial at will, there must be a clear reason for the re-trial as set out in the Code of Civil Procedure as Mentioned above.
Japan bans foreigners from entering country over fears of Kovid-19 virus
TOKYO (Reuters) - The Japanese government has decided to temporarily ban non-permanent residents from entering the country as authorities tighten measures to prevent the spread of the new COVID-19 virus. This is according to the release by CNA on Sunday, December 27, 2020.
The interim measures will take effect on Monday and will be valid throughout January 2021. Overseas Japanese citizens and foreigners who are permanent residents will also be allowed to enter Japan. However, a COVID-19 negative test result must be presented within 72 hours prior to departure and must be validated for two weeks upon arrival.
The decision by the Tokyo government to ban non-permanent residents from entering the country came after the Japanese Ministry of Health announced on Saturday, December 26, 2020 that it had found Early cases of the new COVID-19 virus, which first broke out in the United Kingdom.
