52 Cambodian Workers Camped in the Forest Near Thai-Cambodian Border Deported to Cambodia by Thai Authorities
(Banteay Meanchey): At least 52 Cambodian workers who camped in the jungle along the Cambodian-Thai border to cross the border into Thailand to find work without fear of Kovid-19 have been protected by the Thai military. No. 121, in cooperation with the local sheriff and the Sa Kaeo provincial immigration force, was detained for questioning after receiving reports that the Cambodian workers had smuggled across the border into Thai territory without permission.
The arrest of these Cambodian workers on the evening of December 25, 2020 in the forest of Pa Snguon National Park, under the direct command of the provincial sheriff, Mr. Amret Bon Soya, in order to comply with the order of the head of government to protect and To prevent the spread of Kovid-19, which is rampant in Thailand, these joint forces have launched operations to 14 villages in Nong Muy commune, Kork Khyong district, Sa Kaeo province.
According to the Thai authorities, the Cambodian authorities informed that the camp had 52 workers, including 24 women, 21 men and 7 young children, and sent them to the Cambodian authorities for further action. Cambodian authorities said that the 52 workers were carefully monitored and measured by district health officials, but none of them had a worrying high temperature.
After being questioned, the workers said that they all came from Siem Reap province by crossing the border through the corridors to find agricultural work in Kork Khyong and neighboring districts, earning a day's wages. 200 to 300 baht without having to travel to Bangkok.
