Khema Mushroom Farm prepares food and drink in return for doctors and staff at Chak Angre Health Center who treat Kovid-19 patients
(Phnom Penh): Chak Angre Health Center is the second most important health center in Cambodia after the Khmer-Soviet Friendship Hospital, which acts as a center for the treatment and care of patients with Kovid-19 infection.
In return for this merit, Mr. Tea Sok Chhuon and his wife, the owner of Khema Mushroom Farm (milk mushroom), also prepared food and drinks for the doctors and staff at Chak Angre Health Center on December 25, 2020.
The 14th Chief Medical Officer at Chak Angre Health Center thanked Mr. Tea Sok Chhuon and his wife for providing food and beverage to all doctors and staff.
It should be noted that in case of philanthropists who wish to help or support the front line doctors can come directly in Chak Angre Health Center. Movement for Health Cambodia: Cambodia will increase tax revenue by $ 235 million in five years if tobacco taxes are raised to equal ASEAN countries
(Phnom Penh): Experts from the Cambodian Movement for Health have asked the Royal Government to increase tariffs on tobacco products similar to other ASEAN countries to increase the national tax revenue by 235 million US dollars over five years and cut Reduce mortality and morbidity due to tobacco use.
According to the Cambodian Movement for Health, Cambodia has one of the lowest tobacco tax rates in ASEAN. Currently, the tariff rate on tobacco products compared to the retail price in Cambodia is only 25% for domestic cigarettes and 31.1% for imported cigarettes, while Thailand has a tariff rate compared to the retail price of 70%, Singapore 67.5% and Burma. From 50-60 percent.
Cambodia will receive an additional $ 235 million in additional tax revenue over the next five years and $ 933 million over the next 15 years if Cambodia raises tariffs on tobacco products to 75 percent of the retail price of cigarettes. According to a United Nations study on investment in tobacco control in 2019.
In addition, raising tobacco taxes reduces mortality, morbidity and disability due to the use of tobacco products, in this sense can also reduce the suffering of the people.
Eighty percent of Cambodians support an increase in taxes on tobacco products and an increase in the price of cigarettes (2014 Ministry of Planning National Survey on Adults on Tobacco in Cambodia). In addition, Cambodia is a party to the Convention on Tobacco Control, which is obliged to raise tariffs on tobacco products in order to demonstrate its commitment to the Convention.
Dr. Mom Kong, Executive Director of the Cambodian Movement for Health, stated that "raising taxes on tobacco products is a win-win strategy that gives Cambodia extra income, especially during the Kovid-19 crisis. It can also reduce the side effects caused by the use of tobacco products.
