LDP Complaint to Remove Names from Nearly 3,000 Voters' List Rejected by Constitutional Council
(Phnom Penh): 95 complaints of the League for Democracy Party (LDP) requesting the removal of 2,733 names from the voter list have been resolved by the Constitutional Council as of this morning, December 25, 2020. All 95 LDP complaints were rejected by the Constitutional Council and blocked, and the NEC's decision was upheld.
Of these 95 complaints, 28 were considered by the council on December 21, 23 were resolved on December 23 and 44 were settled on December 25.
Prohm Vichet Akhara, spokesman for the Constitutional Council, told Fresh News that the Constitutional Council had closed all 95 cases of the Coalition for Democracy, which had filed a petition to remove 2,733 names from the initial voter list. This morning, December 25, 2020.
He added, "Of these 95 complaints, the Trial Chamber of the Constitutional Council upheld the decision of the NEC and rejected the LDP's complaint in the spirit of the Constitution, the election law and the law." Relevant.
Representatives of the Coalition for Democracy expressed dissatisfaction with the decision. But for Mr. Prohm Vichet Akhra, the decision of the Constitutional Council is based entirely on the law.
The spokesman added that the decision of the Constitutional Council was a victory for the people, who were on the voter list when they reached voting age.
