South Korea decides to suspend flights from the UK until the end of the year due to concerns over new Kovid-19 virus
SEOUL: South Korean health authorities on Wednesday announced that they have decided to suspend flights from the United Kingdom until the end of the year to prevent the spread of the new COVID-19 virus, which is invading Europe. This is according to the release by the Xinhua news agency on Wednesday night, December 23, 2020.
Yoon Tae-ho, a senior South Korean health official, told a news conference on Wednesday that all flights from the United Kingdom would be suspended from today until December 31 in order to prevent Imported new form of COVID-19 found in the UK. Flights from the western port city of South Korea to London will be resumed.
Under the new rules, all arrivals from the UK will be segregated for 14 days and will be required to be re-tested on a daily basis. Body temperature controls will be tightened and all flight attendants will be tested.
A new form of COVID-19 discovered in the UK is believed to be up to 70% faster. However, there is not much information about this new strain of virus yet, but experts claim that the existing vaccine is still effective. So far, the new coronavirus has not been reported in Asia.
