The cancellation of the hearing by the representatives of the Coalition for Democracy caused the Constitutional Council to reject and block the way to protest the other 23 complaints of the party representatives.
This morning, the hearing on the complaint of the representatives of the Coalition Party for Democracy was chaired by Mr. Im Chhun Lim, President of the Constitutional Council, and other members. The hearing was also attended by representatives of the National Election Committee (NEC), the authority in charge of voter registration.
However, this morning, the representative of the Coalition for Democracy, who is the plaintiff, did not attend the hearing. The absence of the plaintiffs prompted the Constitutional Council to comply with the Constitutional Council's rules of conduct by dismissing these complaints.
A statement from the Constitutional Council stated: "The Trial Chamber of the Constitutional Council has decided to dismiss the 23 complaints as the plaintiffs have dropped their grievances and will not be allowed to stand trial. The way to protest and have the power to apply to all the powers stated in the Constitution and to be published in the government.
According to the NEC, the 19-day voter registration and verification process saw 268,553 new voter registrants, or 89.40 percent of the new voter registration plan. About 300,390 people.
There have been a number of complaints regarding the new voter registration process. The Coalition for Democracy has filed the most lawsuits. Representatives of the Coalition for Democracy have complained about the voter registration process in the communes, claiming that there were irregularities in registration, such as those who did not have an address in the commune who registered but registered.
Complaints by all representatives of the Coalition for Democracy were rejected by the NEC and did not comply with the demands.
Disagreeing with the NEC's decision, representatives of the Coalition for Democracy continued to appeal to the Constitutional Council.
Prohm Vichet Akhara, spokesman for the Constitutional Council, told Fresh News that the Constitutional Council had received about 95 complaints from representatives of the Coalition for Democracy. On December 21, the Constitutional Council held a hearing to resolve 28 cases, on December 23 to resolve 23 cases, and on December 25 to resolve another 44 cases.
The December 21 hearing was attended by representatives of the Coalition for Democracy. During the hearing, the Constitutional Council opposed the demands of the Coalition for Democracy and upheld the NEC's decision and blocked the protest.
The decision of the Constitutional Council on December 21 made the representatives of the Coalition for Democracy dissatisfied and reacted strongly to this decision and therefore they did not attend the hearing on December 23, 2020 This too.
Regarding the reaction of the representative of the Coalition Party for Democracy, Mr. Prohm Vichet Akhara emphasized that the hearing of the Constitutional Council on the decision of the National Election Committee (NEC) on the complaint of the Coalition for Democracy was consistent. By law.
Mr. Prohm Vichet Akhra said that the Constitutional Council works in accordance with the laws and regulations in force. He said that the accusation of the Coalition Party for Democracy about the decision of the Constitutional Council is unfair and transparent, it is against the law.
Takeo Provincial Police Commissioner is investigating and disciplining four police officers from the Anti-Drug Bureau after allegedly stealing more than $ 7,000 from residents
