Australian ambassador tells Commerce Minister to increase scholarships for Cambodian students
To begin with, the Minister and Ambassador discussed the Kovid-19 crisis, which has had a lasting impact on the daily lives of all people, and welcomed the signing of the Comprehensive Economic Partnership Agreement. Regions (RCEP) of which Cambodia and Australia became members last November.
During the meeting, the Minister requested the Australian side to provide Consumer Protection technical officers stationed in Cambodia to share experiences and improve work efficiency to increase the volume of Cambodian products exported to Australia and requested the Australian side to continue. Support scholarships for Cambodia. He also invited Australian e-commerce companies to invest in Cambodia.
In response, the Ambassador informed the Minister that Australia would continue to provide scholarships to Cambodia and also praised Cambodia despite the difficult situation in the Kovid-19 crisis, the export of Cambodian jasmine rice to Australia increased by about 60%.
The Ambassador also briefed Minister Pan Sosak on the Australian-backed CAVAC project, which trained about 2,000 Cambodian farmers in the electronics sector to sell their products online.
At the 18th meeting of the Cambodia-Vietnam Joint Commission, the two sides noted the traditional strong friendship, good-neighborliness and long-term stability, as well as the further enhancement of the comprehensive strategic partnership between the two countries. The two highlighted through the telephone conversations and video systems of the leaders and the exchange of visits by senior leaders in the past.
The two sides commended the effective cooperation and mutual support in preventing and combating the Kovid-19 epidemic while striving to maintain cross-border trade flows and investment. .
The two sides agreed to strengthen cooperation and mutual support at the regional and international levels, continue to coordinate in the fight against transnational crime, planned crime and cybercrime, and improve mechanisms for information exchange. In all areas.
For cooperation in the field of education and human resources, Mr. Prak Sokhon thanked his Vietnamese counterpart for facilitating the return of Cambodian students to Vietnam to continue their studies. Both sides agreed to continue to provide mutual scholarships to students for the academic year 2020 and 2021.
In terms of cooperation in agriculture, forestry and fisheries, the two sides reaffirmed their strong commitment and joint efforts to control illegal exploitation, transportation and trade in illegal timber and wildlife. Cross the border. The two sides also agreed to work closely to strengthen cooperation in animal and plant hygiene, fisheries, rubber, fruits, beef, pork and irrigation.
The two sides reaffirmed their commitment to work together to successfully implement environmental, water resources and meteorological projects. The two sides agreed to increase cooperation in the efficient and sustainable use, management and development of the Mekong River water resources, as well as to promote the protection and conservation of the environment, climate-related activities, green growth and land management.
For energy cooperation, the two sides agreed to explore opportunities for trade cooperation in the energy sector and joint research on clean and renewable energy development.
After the 18th Meeting of the Cambodia-Vietnam Joint Commission, Mr. Prak Sokhon and Mr. Pham Binh Minh signed the Minutes of the 18th Meeting of the Cambodia-Vietnam Joint Commission and exchanged the instruments of ratification of the Supplementary Treaty. On the 1985 Treaty on the Demarcation of the State Boundary and the 2005 Supplementary Treaty and the Protocol on the Boundary and Land Boundary between the Kingdom of Cambodia and the Socialist Republic of Vietnam.
