(Phnom Penh): Mr. Say Sam Al, Minister of Environment, encouraged farmers in protected areas to participate in the implementation of the Friendship Rice Project to save wildlife more actively. He also asked WCS and SMP to expand the project to other areas and urged Cambodians in the country to support the Wildlife Friendly Rice.
The Minister of Environment made the announcement on the occasion that he and the Ministry's leadership presided over the official inauguration of the IBIS Rice Conservation Group in Phnom Penh's Phsar Doeum Thkov district and inspected the packaging of the Wildlife Rescue Friendship Organization. Of the company.
IBIS Rice was established by the Wildlife Conservation Association in 2017 to fulfill the potential of this product and increase production.
IBIS Rice currently works with 500 wildlife rescue farmers, offering a purchase price that is 150% higher than the market price for Rumduol-friendly organic rice. Farmers have vowed not to cut down trees, hunt wildlife or use toxic chemicals to protect protected areas and rare species in the area.
Nick Spencer, CEO of IBIS Rice, said: "Customers support and value our wildlife-friendly rice products and quality because they are organic. However, the main reason why customers buy our rice is because it is linked to the conservation and improvement of farmers' livelihoods.
According to Ken Serey Ratha, Director of the Cambodian Wildlife Conservation Society, “Achieving conservation requires us to work closely with communities in the wildlife sanctuary to ensure that their goals are met in a timely manner. Wildlife sanctuaries around areas where communities live are also protected. "The Wildlife Friendship Rice Project could be a global recognition."
The giant trout is the largest bird in the world, and because it is a rare bird, bird watchers and conservationists think it is almost non-existent. In this world.
Giant trout are classified as the most endangered species, especially due to their disturbing effects on humans and hunting. The remaining giant trout have gathered to live in the northern part of Cambodia. In 2009, WCS, with the support of the Royal Government of Cambodia, established the Abis Rice Project as a way to support this type of conservation.
The project aims to encourage local communities to participate in conservation by purchasing organic rice at the highest price from a group of farmers who are committed to protecting the forest and stopping wildlife hunting.
The project contributes to improving the livelihoods of target farmers while simultaneously addressing key threats to the most endangered species, including giant trout and their habitat, through deforestation. Wet land used as rice field for rice cultivation.
This rice is recognized as an organic rice and contributes to wildlife rescue according to EU and US Department of Agriculture market standards. This rice is currently sold in the world market at high prices, including markets in Europe and Canada. The project has contributed to improving the livelihoods of target farmers as well as reducing deforestation by 75 percent in areas where farmers live and grow crops.
The quality of Pka Rumduol rice and the parallel story mean that the demand for Abis rice in the international market exceeds the supply. As IBIS recruits and trains more farmers, the company needs more working capital to increase the volume of rice purchases.
