During December of each year, a time when fish begin to emerge from the Tonle Sap Lake down the Tonle Sap River and into other areas. During this time, it is also the time when Cambodians start to think about collecting fish to make prahok, just to eat it.
For 2020, the Fisheries Administration has announced that fishing will start to decline from the Tonle Sap Lake between December 20 and 26, and called on people who like to make prahok to be ready to buy fish to Make prahok, too.
On the banks of the Tonle Sap River in Ponhea Leu District, Kandal Province, people from the provinces of Kandal, Kampong Speu and Kampong Chhnang often gather to buy fish for prahok.
This year, too, people from the provinces came together to buy prahok fish. View at this prahok gathering place Some people are not able to buy prahok fish as planned. Not as planned because the fish are not as plentiful as every year and are also expensive.
During the inspection of prahok and fishing for prahok on December 22, 2020, Mr. Veng Sakhon, Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, said that the harvest of fish for prahok this year has Decrease from about 13 percent to 30 percent.
Minister Veng Sakhon claimed that the decline in freshwater fish production was due to changes in water levels during the rainy season this year, which was too low for fish to breed.
Although the number of fish for prahok is declining in the year, the minister emphasized that there is still enough to make prahok, but there is still enough. The minister also expressed expectations that fish prices would fall as fish prices plummeted.
The minister called on people to make more prahok and prahok in this and the next during January 2021.
On this occasion, the Minister of Agriculture also pointed out that although this year the natural freshwater fish has decreased, but not a problem of lack of fish for people to eat. This shortage is due to the fact that the amount of fish raised has increased to about 30 percent, which can come to meet the eating needs of the people.
Mr. Veng Sakhon said that this figure clearly indicates that the aquaculture sub-sector has been playing an important role in supplying the local market instead of catfish and fishery products in the fishing area in accordance with Samdech's wishes. Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Cambodia, has launched a plan to boost food production through fish farming.
