The International Court of Justice has the role of resolving disputes between one nation and another. For example, the case of the Temple of Preah Vihear between Cambodia and Thailand. The tribunal is also responsible for resolving violations and interpreting treaties.
The International Court of Justice is a council of 15 judges elected by the General Assembly and the Security Council for a nine-year term. Judges from all nationalities of the member countries have the right to serve in this court, but in each mandate they are not allowed to have two judges of the same nationality working at the same time.
Although the International Court of Justice is a major body of the United Nations, it is not located in New York City, as is the United Nations.
The International Criminal Court (ICC) began its operation on July 1, 2002, when the Rome Statute, a multilateral treaty, served as the basic document and function of the tribunal. Most of the funding for the tribunal comes from member countries, and some comes from UN contributions, as well as from international organizations and companies.
The member countries of the International Criminal Organization are those that have ratified the Rome Statute. As of 2019, there are 123 member states of this court.
Please be informed that the ICC has up to 18 judges and these 18 judges are elected for a period of 9 years by the member countries of this tribunal. Each judge candidate must also be a national of those member countries.
As for the role of the International Criminal Court, it has no jurisdiction or jurisdiction over disputes between countries, but has the power to prosecute individuals who commit serious crimes such as genocide, crimes against humanity, and crimes against humanity. War and so on. For example, the Extraordinary Chambers in the Courts of Cambodia, which tried former leaders during the Democratic Kampuchea era.
According to the procedure of the International Criminal Court, the ICC can only open an investigation into a case if it notifies the court directly, providing reliable information, meaning that there is accurate evidence of the crime. Any that. Screams for help through radio or newspaper broadcasts can sometimes go unreported to the courts for legal action.
It should be noted that the International Court of Justice and the International Criminal Court have also been criticized for the transparency of the courts in resolving disputes involving a number of superpowers, as they have received contributions from those superpowers. In addition, some countries have withdrawn their membership from the two international courts.
