Cambodia-Vietnam exchange of instruments of ratification of the 2019 Supplementary Treaty and the border demarcation protocol
(Phnom Penh): The governments of Cambodia and Vietnam on December 22, 2020, have already exchanged the instruments of ratification of the 2019 Supplementary Treaty and the Khan Seima Protocol to establish a land border post between the two countries' borders. This was confirmed by Ms. Koy Pisey, Deputy Director of the Permanent Secretariat of the National Authority for Border Affairs to Fresh News.
The exchange of instruments took place on the morning of December 22, 2020, on the occasion of Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon, Minister of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation, co-chairing with Deputy Prime Minister Pham Binh Minh, Minister of Foreign Affairs of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam. The 18th Cambodia-Vietnam Joint Commission Meeting via Video.
It should be noted that the exchange of instruments of ratification of the 2019 Supplementary Treaty and the Protocol on the demarcation of the land border is a legal bond between the two countries, Cambodia and Vietnam, which must be respected and implemented under the framework of international law.
The Draft Law on the Approval of the Protocol for the Demarcation of Khan Boundary and Land Border between the Kingdom of Cambodia and the Socialist Republic of Vietnam was promulgated by the King of Cambodia on November 25, 2020.
Ms. Koy Pisey said that so far, there are three provinces in Cambodia and have completed the demarcation of the border out of the nine provinces bordering Cambodia-Vietnam. The three provinces that have completed the border demarcation are Kratie, Tbong Khmum and Prey Veng, while the remaining six provinces are committed by the two governments in the near future.
