(Siem Reap): With the coordination of the Siem Reap Provincial Administration, 405 families living along the Siem Reap River from the point of the Dragon Bridge to the Angkor Bridge in Sangkat Slor Kram, Siem Reap City, volunteered to demolish their old buildings. And agreed to move to Run Ta Ek Natural Village, Khun Ream Commune, Banteay Srei District.
This volunteering took place after the Siem Reap Provincial Administration arranged for 405 families to participate in the lottery and thumbprint to accept plots of land in Run Ta Ek Natural Village on December 19, 2020. According to the principle of the provincial administration, the land provided to the people is 20 meters by 30 meters.
In order to coordinate with the people more accurately, Mr. Tea Seiha, Governor of Siem Reap Province, on December 21, 2020, led the team to inspect the location and land preparation for the above people. Along with inspecting this location, the Governor instructed the technical team to properly install the border posts, water and electricity systems and prepare the infrastructure to suit the needs of the people.
The people were happy to accept this policy and thanked the Royal Government of Cambodia and the provincial leaders for taking care of the people.
Due to the current situation, which is worsening the economic crisis, the people also requested the provincial administration to help insure and transport their construction to the new location.
Please be informed that these activities are the participation of the people in the development of Siem Reap through the provision of 38 infrastructures and the restoration of the Siem Reap River, a gift from Samdech Techo Prime Minister to enhance the beauty of Siem Reap. The smart city ahead. It is also an attraction for national and international tourists to visit in large numbers after Kovid-19 is over.
"Plastic rice exchange" project to make communities stop littering
(Siem Reap): The Plastic Rice Exchange Project of the Bamboo Vine Foundation has changed the behavior of more than 300 families living in the floating village of Keo Por commune, Puok district, by stopping the dumping of plastic waste into water or incineration. Garbage continues, because there has been a barn for proper garbage storage.
Sea Sophal, President of the Bamboo Shoots Foundation, The program focuses on collecting only plastic waste, because plastic is hundreds of years old, which poses a high risk to both human and animal life. People who participate in this program will receive incentives in the form of rice, budget and food.
He added that due to the success of the program and the support of the people as the next target, the Tampaing Bamboo Foundation will continue its project along the Siem Reap River and in many other villages in Siem Reap and Battambang. Also.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State and spokesman for the Ministry of Environment, said that Cambodia has more than 10,000 tons of garbage per day, with nearly 4 million tons per year. Of that, 65 to 70 percent is organic waste, 20 percent is plastic waste, and the rest is solid waste. Garbage in Cambodia is growing between 10 and 15 percent every year due to economic growth, people's packaging behavior, limited processing and increased consumption.
He said that the Ministry of Environment is paying attention to and promoting the separation of waste into categories such as: organic waste to be used as compost and plastic waste to be recycled. The ministry is also pushing for the implementation of 4R principles, including: reducing the use of plastics, recycling, reusing and banning the use of plastics.
A spokesman for the Ministry of Environment said that the purpose of establishing a plastic barn and a plastic rice exchange project is to reduce the use of plastic or reduce plastic pollution in the Tonle Sap Lake and also to protect the biodiversity in the lake. Tonle Sap. Plastic is a chemical residue and does not easily mix with water or soil.
Ms. Tim Oun, 59 years old, a resident of Peam Ta Ou Village, Keo Por Commune, Puok District, expressed her support for the project that the Viney Grape Foundation Foundation, in collaboration with the local authorities, has set up this project to collect plastic waste, which has made her community Beautiful, no more plastic litter floating on the water or on the branches.
She added that although the project will be completed at some point, on behalf of the people, he will continue to collect or sort garbage properly for the health of the people in the community as a whole.
In addition, Ms. Tim Oun stated that since following the instructions of this organization, her community has been more beautiful than before, while not collecting garbage, her community is full. Garbage, plastic bags and water bottles float all over the Tonle Sap Lake.
On the other hand, Ms. Mao Pan, a volunteer of Tampaing Snong Russey organization, said that the reason she joined this volunteer activity was because she felt that Tonle Sap Lake was a tourist area, but in the communities on the surface of Tonle Sap Lake, it was full. With the garbage floating on the water at this point, she and other friends participated in inspiring the Tonle Sap activities without plastic garbage to the people in the community from 2017 until now.
Ms. Mao Pan said that the people living in this community have a better understanding of how to clean, store and sort plastic waste, how to use and store garbage.
Ms. Ear Samnang, a young volunteer from Peam Ta Or village, Keo Por commune, Puok district, said that through this program, the Tonle Sap Lake without plastic bags in her community is more beautiful than before. There are bamboo vines to educate the community, the surface of the water is full of rubbish, and when the water recedes, the rubbish is scattered all over the land, causing loss of environmental beauty and disease. And so on.
She added that the garbage problem is not the responsibility of anyone or any group alone. Therefore, in addition to the work of government authorities, every citizen must also contribute to this work, starting from giving up old habits, stop throwing garbage in the open and creating new habits.
