General Vong Pisen Holds a Bilateral Meeting with the Commander-in-Chief of the Royal Thai Army via Video
(Phnom Penh): General Vong Pisen, Commander-in-Chief of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces, on the morning of December 22, 2020, held a bilateral meeting with General Chalem Pol Srisawaddy, Commander-in-Chief of the Royal Thai Armed Forces, via video.
Speaking on the occasion, General Vong Pisen emphasized that the bilateral meeting demonstrated the commitment and compliance with the spirit of the agreement reached at the 14th Cambodia-Thailand General Boundary Commission (GBC) meeting. Together to establish an annual meeting to strengthen relations and cooperation between the two armies.
This is the 70th anniversary of the establishment of diplomatic relations between Cambodia and Thailand, and the good relations and cooperation between the two countries will improve the situation along the border. This is the desired goal of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Cambodia, in turning the border with neighboring countries into a border of peace, friendship, cooperation and development.
General Vong Pisen thanked the Royal Thai Army for its past contribution to the development of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces (RCAF) through its training assistance, including demining cooperation and border crime prevention.
The Commander-in-Chief also thanked Pheap Thai for providing information on the 19 Thai Kovid epidemic, facilitating Cambodians to cross the border to receive medical treatment and to accept Cambodian legal workers to work in Thailand. Back.
He hoped that the two armies would continue to work closely to exchange intelligence and expand cooperation, and hold more joint exercises, especially on security and disaster relief.
In response, General Chalerm Pol Srivawadi stressed that in the current situation, due to the 19 cases of communicable disease, Thailand has noted cross-border travel between the two countries, in which the Thai side has facilitated access to treatment for Cambodians. Disease despite border closures.
"So far, Thailand has taken strict measures to prevent the spread of the disease to Cambodian workers and asked Cambodia to have confidence in this action in the event of 19 cases of Kovid," he said. Will be kept separate for security in Thailand.
On that occasion, on behalf of the Commander-in-Chief of the Royal Thai Army, General Chalerm Polsri Sawatdy thanked the Cambodian Ministry of National Defense for providing scholarships to Thai trainees to study the Khmer language every year.
