Phnom Kulen is an invaluable natural heritage pool
(Siem Reap): Preah Jayavarman-Norodom National Park "Phnom Kulen" is an important historical site that preserves many types of historical buildings, such as ancient temples, artefacts, forest inscriptions, natural water systems and communities. According to the study of Stok Kak Thom inscription, Phnom Kulen is an ancient capital built during the reign of King Jayavarman II in the early 9th century under the name "Mahendrapot".
Phnom Kulen is an area that provides a source of water for irrigation, which flows along the historical river of Siem Reap to feed people, animals, plants, important agricultural work and so on. We must protect the environment, natural resources for our lives and livelihoods.
Jayavarman-Norodom National Park "Phnom Kulen" has the potential to supply fresh water and groundwater for Siem Reap, especially as a groundwater source for maintaining ancient temples, mostly built on sand dunes.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra continued that the Ministry of Environment and stakeholders, especially at the sub-national level, which are the implementers of PES at the provincial level, have decided on the mechanism of implementation and fundraising by choosing the appropriate option to implement PES in Phnom Kulen. Voluntary contributions from large water users (businesses); Second, payment of commercial water use, including producers and distributors.
Ministry of Environment spokesman Net Pheaktra stressed that in order to sustain ecosystem services in Phnom Kulen National Park, especially water from Phnom Kulen, it is necessary to protect and preserve Phnom Kulen ecosystems, such as preventing forest loss, rehabilitation. Restore lost forests and natural water systems to prevent pollution of rivers and groundwater.
Mr. Sakhoeun Sakda added that while on the ridge of Kulen Mountain, there are ancient pools and dams with an area of more than 100 hectares. Geographically, Phnom Kulen is located in the heart of Siem Reap province. Irrigate various crops and flow to the Tonle Sap Lake as well.
In addition, he stated that there are three major rivers that flow from Phnom Kulen for water distribution: Stung Rolous in the east of the province, Stung Siem Reap in the central part of the province and Stung Puok in the west of the province. . In addition to water sources, Phnom Kulen is rich in heritage sites with more than 40 temples, as well as some other temples that experts have not yet found.
