The warning to take legal action by the Ministry of Environment and relevant authorities against the perpetrators of the theft of wild hair was issued after some criminals continue to buy wild hair, a rare species in Cambodia. To continue exporting to traders in neighboring countries.
A spokesman for the Ministry of Environment said: "The Ministry of Environment would like to warn the big business people who destroy the forest, we will take legal action because the Ministry of Environment has already warned those who do business in the forest. "Excessive to the point of devastation, we do not understand them."
The senior official of the Ministry of Environment also stated that in Cambodia, there are five areas with wild hair species, including: Cardamom Mountains, Central lowlands, highlands, coastal areas and islands and mountains. In addition, there are two areas affected by the commercial hair trade: the Cardamom Mountains and the highlands.
Related to this, Mr. Neth Pheaktra added that wild hair is a rare natural resource and belongs to Cambodia that needs to be maintained and preserved. The ban or ban on the trading of corals also comes as experts have been studying the exact number of wild corals present in Cambodia.
According to the spokesman, the young activist also claimed: "While the experts are studying, we do not have a clear number of genera, so if there is a collection or business of wild hair, it "It is possible to lose our species that other countries can trade or own, so we need to protect, preserve and prevent illegal trade."
He added that in the past, some crooks have been spreading information on social media about the high price of wildlife, which has led to the anarchic procurement of wildlife from protected areas. Affects endemic species and can lead to the complete extinction of some species.
According to Chan Somaly, Undersecretary of State at the Ministry of Environment, the Sok An Phnom Kulen Center for Research and Conservation has recently identified 170 species of wild corals that are estimated to be endemic. Between 500 and 700 species across the country.
Please refer to the Law on Protected Areas, Article 59, in points 3 and 4, which states that the transportation and harvesting of natural resource by-products without permission and the collection of by-products of natural resources without permission, and subject to transaction fines in cash. 1 million to 10 million riels or to repair, restore damage and confiscate exhibits as state property.
Article 61, point 4, stipulates that cutting, tying, pruning, felling, collecting, destroying, encroaching and stockpiling endangered or rare and endangered plants as defined by the Prakas of the Ministry of Environment shall be punished as natural resource offenses. Third, which can be imprisoned from 1 to 5 years and / or a fine of 15 million to 150 million riels.
