Understand the law: non-recidivism
(Phnom Penh): The Penal Code of the Kingdom of Cambodia is a collection of criminal provisions in which the overall content focuses on the notion of criminal offenses to be punished, as well as some criminal rules. The purpose of establishing this criminal provision is to punish those who have committed acts that endanger society as well as to prevent the recurrence of crimes.
There is also a special provision that plays an important role in preventing the recurrence of criminal offenses: the provision of non-recidivism, which means that a person has been convicted of a crime and that person has committed a crime. Another offense in a period of time is entered into a state of non-recidivism, which will be aggravated to the new offense.
So, to be more detailed, what is non-recidivism? And what kind of criminal punishment will be given to the person who commits immoral acts?
FRESH NEWS Legal Awareness Program in collaboration with the Cambodian Bar Association for International Cooperation This week, the topic of "non-recidivism" will be presented to the following students:
The provisions on non-recidivism are set out in the Penal Code of the Kingdom of Cambodia, in which the general notion of non-recidivism refers to a situation in which a person has committed a crime or misdemeanor and has been convicted. Committing another felony or misdemeanor within a period prescribed by the Penal Code.
