Brother shot his brother to death in a pool of blood in Takhmao City, Kandal Province because he was angry when he stopped him while arguing with his wife
(Kandal): A gunman shot his brother dead in a pool of blood and escaped on the morning of December 19, 2020 in Prek Khsev village, Roka Khpos commune, Takhmao city, Kandal province.
According to a report from the Kandal Provincial Police, the victim, the brother of the gunman, Chhay Siemmey, over 43 years old, was an electrician living in Kahe Village, Kahe Commune, Kang Meas District, Kampong Cham Province. The assailant, Chhay Salap, 36, lived at the scene.
Authorities identified the suspect as a drug user and often shared the same name with his wife selling products online. On the afternoon of December 18, 2020, the suspect had an argument with his wife, when the victim came to help stop the quarrel, but then the suspect became angry that the victim knew about the couple. They want to break up their marriage.
At 4:00 am on the day of the incident, the suspect called the victim from Kampong Cham province to come to his house in Prek Khsev village, Roka Khpos commune, Takhmao city, Kandal province to talk for a while. At that time, the victim rode a motorcycle to meet the suspect by appointment, but when he arrived, he did not say anything. Severe injuries.
According to the detailed report of the department on December 18, 2020, Sre Nong commune, Tram Kak district, Takeo province, is a commune where many farmers and families like to raise ducks and incubate duck eggs in the traditional way. Considering that can help increase the income in their family life better.
Seeing that this business has a high income and a lot of market, especially in Phnom Penh, his family has increased the incubation from year to year. Until 2016, the General Department of Animal Health and Animal Production, in cooperation with the Provincial Department of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, launched a training course on duck raising techniques and methods of incubating duck eggs using electricity.
Gained experience, he started using an electric duck egg incubator that can hold 9,000 duck eggs in 4 days. Step by step, the success has improved. To date, he has two incubators that can lay 300,000 eggs per month. He can sell 3 types of products:
He added that in the context of the global epidemic of Kovid-19, many villagers did not go out to work and returned to raising ducks. Therefore, it is a good opportunity for him to expand this business further by recruiting unemployed villagers to work with him.
With enough labor today, he can sell 80,000-100,000 duck eggs a day, the largest market being Phnom Penh. At present, there are 27 duck egg incubators in the commune and 56 families raising ducks, including 12 husk incubators and 15 electric incubators, and they have formed an incubator business association. The duck eggs are already there.
(Phnom Penh): Ms. Tanabe Kaori, named in Khmer as Kunthea, First Secretary of the Embassy of Japan in Cambodia, has completed her mission in Cambodia after completing her second work in Cambodia for 4 years. Half a year (2016-2020).
