In ancient times, Cambodia produced copper for domestic supply and export to neighboring countries.
(Siem Reap): The implementation of the project "LANGAU-Set to the King" has led the team of APSARA Authority to find many small ancient remnants and find many copper production sites in the northwest corner of Angkor Thom for Supplying local copper products in ancient times and exporting them to neighboring countries.
The implementation of the project "LANGAU-Set to the King" began in 2016, a study of the ancient metalwork of the bronze workshop in the Royal Palace of Angkor Thom during the 11th to 12th centuries to determine the source of Supply of copper elements both in ancient Cambodia and exported to neighboring countries.
This excavation was carried out in collaboration with the Apsara Authority and the French School of the Far East (EFEO) and involved a team of archaeologists, archaeologists, geologists, pottery experts, and pottery experts. Science and International Metal Repair Specialist.
Excavations at the site from 2016 to the present have uncovered a number of remnants, including 20 coal-fired furnaces, metal smelters and metal smelters, kiln walls, bulldozers, pedestals, and set stoves. Metals and so on. According to a 7,500-square-meter geochemical survey, heavy metal pollution could also help experts understand the location of the plant and its impacts in the vicinity. .
Recent analytical data and the results of other technical studies, especially on metal statues, show the diversity of the blacksmiths in ancient palaces, allowing modern researchers to understand the lifestyle and design of The whole metal handicraft factory in the Khmer administration during the Angkorian period.
To inspect the above work, on December 17, 2020, Mr. Hang Pov, Director General of APSARA Authority, together with his colleagues, inspected the above excavation site. Minister of Post calls on bankrupt people to consider learning skills in ICT and digital telecommunications technology
