A boy died of Kampot fish poisoning after his grandfather cooked it for him to eat in Koh Kong province
(Koh Kong): A boy died because his grandfather fished Kampot fish from the sea and cooked it for his grandson to eat at dinner. This case happened at 1 am on December 18, 2020 in Village 3, Sangkat Smach Meanchey, Cam Phoumin City, Koh Kong Province.
The victim was an 11-year-old boy named Krin who lived with his grandfather. The victim's grandfather, 67-year-old Ta Min, is a sofa repairman, while his grandmother, 67-year-old Yeay Mao, works as a babysitter. The child was born to a migrant mother in Thailand.
The grandfather of the boy, Ta Min, said that due to his poor family life, in the evening he took his grandson on a bicycle to go fishing on the shores of Prek Koh Por. He caught two Kampot fish and returned home to cook.
Ministry of Environment and Kampot Provincial Administration held a meeting to discuss the land of the people who actually enjoy the land in the protected area of "Preah Monivong Bokor" National Park
(Kampot): Mr. Sot Ya, Undersecretary of State, Ministry of Environment and Deputy Chairman of the Working Group on Human Identity and Land Identification in Kampot Province, and Mr. Chiao Tay, Governor of Kampot Province, chaired a meeting to discuss the land use and enjoyment of the people. Apparently in the protected area of "Monivong Bokor National Park". This meeting was held at Kampot Provincial Hall on December 18, 2020 with the participation of many stakeholders.
Mr. Suy Thea, Director of the Provincial Department of Environment, reported at the meeting that a total of 3,386 families had applied for the survey on 3,913 plots of land measuring 12,247 hectares.
There are 1413 forest plots equal to 5599 hectares occupied by 1248 families, so according to the above results, only 2,138 families have actually enjoyed and occupied 2,500 plots of land of the proposed size. And 6,649 hectares in Monivong Bokor National Park, which the team will measure and issue a title deed to.
Mr. Soth Ya, Undersecretary of State of the Ministry of Environment and Deputy Chairman of the Working Group on Human Identity and Land Identification in Kampot Province, commented on the key ingredients for the meeting to find solutions, including:
* First: Land that people have actually cultivated and enjoyed for a long time * Second: Forest land without crops * Third: Land in Bokor Park and in dispute * Fourth: Forest land Recently, after the discussion of stakeholders, the meeting decided to set out some principles for the working group to implement, including:
Chiao Tai Provincial Governor said that the work of clearing land for the people is very important so that the people who have been occupying the land and enjoying it for a long time can get a title deed and be able to use all their land. Possibility and potential without fear, and the provision of this title in order to contribute to reducing poverty and improving the lives of citizens.
