(Phnom Penh): Ms. O Vandin, Secretary of State and spokesperson of the Ministry of Health, on December 16, 2020, issued a statement stating that "Cambodia has ordered 1 million doses of Kovid 19 vaccine, but not a product. China.
Ms. O Vandin's dismissal of this information by the newspaper wrote a news article in violation of the statement made by Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Cambodia on December 15, 2020. The Ministry of Health considered the article misleading and requested the author to correct it immediately.
A spokesman for the Ministry of Health added that it was not yet known when Cambodia would receive the vaccine, depending on when the Kovak Center would receive 19 Kovid vaccines, and the World Health Organization had evaluated the vaccine. That's the effectiveness and quality of the vaccine to be ready before the distribution.
Therefore, if a vaccine manufacturer from any country, including China, can produce the final product and supply it to the Kovak Center with prior approval, then the World Health Organization has already assessed its effectiveness and quality. Equality of distribution will be based on the actual number of vaccines distributed to the member countries of the Kovak Center.
While rejecting the information, Ms. O Vandin requested that there be no explanation or interpretation that is contrary to the principles that Cambodia has adhered to, especially not to misrepresent Samdech Techo Hun Sen. She said that if any media wants to broadcast the speech of Samdech Techo Prime Minister, please raise any point completely to avoid misspellings, misinterpretations or any possible confusion.
"I call for immediate correction on this issue to avoid any confusion or provocation on bilateral or multilateral cooperation with friendly countries." This is the confirmation of the spokesperson of the Ministry of Health.
During a special message on December 15, 2020, Samdech Techo Hun Sen emphasized that Cambodia only buys vaccines from countries recognized by the World Health Organization. Samdech Techo also asked for understanding from the country that produces the vaccine to confirm his position. He added that so far no vaccine has been recognized by the World Health Organization.
