The World Bank said in a statement that while there has been some easing in social security measures, domestic economic activity is slowly returning to normal. The government's unprecedented level of intervention has helped in part to keep the consumption sector stable and keep inflation under control.
According to the report, the inflow of foreign direct investment into non-garment and agricultural projects continues to increase, most likely due to the attractiveness of Cambodia's free trade agreements with countries in the region.
Although total exports of pharmaceutical products have been affected by the epidemic, growth has continued in exports of other factory products, including bicycles, electrical components, and auto parts and components. Exports of rice and other agricultural products remain strong. At the same time, domestic tourism has helped in part to revitalize the tourism and travel industry.
"Cambodia's growth prospects remain very uncertain," said Ingona Dobraja, the World Bank Country Manager for Cambodia. "The most important goal of the current policy is to urgently restore jobs lost or suspended due to the epidemic."
"Going forward, it is necessary to encourage domestic and foreign investment to reap the benefits of the recently established bilateral and regional free trade agreements," she said. "This is possible through the rapid introduction of competitive investment laws and investment incentives, as well as measures to facilitate business."
The report also highlighted opportunities for facilitating the expansion of domestic and foreign investment based on the recent bilateral and regional free trade agreements, including: a free trade agreement between Cambodia and China; Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership Agreement (RCEP).
The report also points to the urgency of building a strong infrastructure for Kovid 19 vaccine, as well as a close monitoring of the economic vulnerabilities that can be caused by the long-term growth of the construction sector. Real estate.
