In 2017, Russia and Turkey signed a $ 2.5 billion deal to buy S-400 air defense systems from Moscow. As of July 2019, the Turkish government is still pushing for an agreement, despite US sanctions banning the purchase of military equipment from Russia.
Earlier Monday, the US Treasury Department announced sanctions against four Turkish officials, relevant Turkish institutions, the head of the defense industry, as well as the military equipment auction agency. The measures are in response to the Turkish government's decision to buy a missile defense system from Russia in defiance of US and allied calls.
The agreement violates US sanctions. The Sanctions Act (CAATSA) was enacted to counter US enemies targeting Russia, North Korea and Iran and barred countries from purchasing intelligence or military equipment from Moscow. And if any country does not comply, it may face sanctions.
