Japanese Government Provides Nearly $ 1 Million in Grants to Two Projects in Cambodia (Video inside) (Phnom Penh): The Royal Government of Japan, through its Embassy in Cambodia, has agreed to provide a total of $ 968,390 in grants to two Cambodian organizations. To strengthen the pediatric surgical care system in Kratie province and to promote integrated education in Kandal province after the grant scheme for the Japanese NGO project.
The signing ceremony was held on December 14, 2020 by Mr. Mikami Masahiro, Ambassador Extraordinary and Plenipotentiary of Japan to Cambodia and a representative of both recipients.
The Japanese government-funded projects include:
In this project, FIDR aims to improve the pediatric surgical care system in Kratie province by training the medical staff of the provincial referral department, health centers and village health volunteers in Kratie province. To improve the quality of his services and create a safer treatment facility, FIDR will also build surgical facilities for patients, especially pediatric patients, including newborns.
In this project, AAR Japan will establish an assessment system on environmental education and implementation of activities to support the capacity building of the Disability Support Committee at the commune administration level. Through this project, the learning environment and quality of education for children with disabilities in Cambodia as a whole will be improved.
