Deadline! Thailand has not yet dismantled landmines along the border in Thmor Puok district, Banteay Meanchey province, Major General Seng Thearin ordered the forces stationed in the area to dismantle.
(Banteay Meanchey): The deadline has passed, but the Thai side did not respond to the request of the Cambodian military to remove the mine mark, therefore, on the evening of December 12, 2020, Major General Seng Thearin, Commander The Deputy Chief of Staff of the 5th Military Zone ordered the forces stationed at the border post No. 32-33 in Samaki Village, Kork Romiet Commune, Thmor Puok District, Banteay Meanchey Province to remove all mine signs.
Major General Seng Thearin's ultimatum was issued on December 9, 2020 to the Thai military in a meeting attended by Major General Prum Roeun and Major General Tim Tum, Deputy Chief of the Land Border Police Department. During the meeting, the general ordered the Thai side to remove all mine signs and red lines from the area between the 32nd and 33rd posts.
It should be noted that on December 9, 2020, Major General Seng Thearin informed the Cambodian-Thai border liaison team to inform the Thai side to remove all mine signs within two days from today. If the demining signs are not dismantled in two days, the forces stationed there will dismantle themselves and all activities along the border, let the Thai side inform each other.
