Ratanakkiri Provincial Governor Leads a Working Group to Investigate the Impact of People Living Around Kanseng Lake in Infrastructure Development Project Around Boeung Kanseng
(Ratanakkiri): On the morning of December 14, 2020, Mr. Thong Savun, Governor of Ratanakkiri Province, led a relevant working group to inspect the impact on the people living around Kanseng Lake, Banlung City in the project to improve infrastructure development around the lake.
As a result, a total of 45 families were affected in this project at the actual level (touching the fences, stalls and porches of houses built on the side of the road), of which 44 families applauded the project by willingly cooperating and participating with the provincial authorities. Dismantle stalls and porches from the sidewalk to allow authorities to build infrastructure.
After Mr. Thong Savun's mediation, explaining in detail the vision and development project of the province and identifying the actual impact, Ms. Khay Chenda agreed to accept and cooperate with the provincial authorities, but she demanded compensation for some of the impacts (affecting parts of the hotel fence). ).
Responding to the above Facebook account, Banlung Mayor Ngin Nel said that the province has a clear plan to develop and expand the road around Boeung Kanseng Lake. Prior to the expansion, the Banlung City Administration issued a notice to 45 families living around Boeung Kanseng Lake to remove their verandas or fences for 30 days.
He said that after informing the people have good cooperation with the provincial committee as well as with the municipal administration. Separately, the hotel owner refused to cooperate in dismantling the hotel fence built into the public road of the state.
Mr. Ngin Nel added that the Banlung City Administration has now issued a notice to Ms. Khay Chenda, the hotel owner, giving her another 15 days from December 11, 2020 to remove the fence or affected building.
