General Knowledge: Understanding the African Union
1: About the African Union
The African Union covers an area of about 29 million square kilometers, including the famous lands of the Sahara and the Nile. Official languages include Arabic, English, French, Portuguese, Spanish, Swahili, and some African languages. In the African Union, there are a number of official bodies, such as the Peace and Security Council and its parliament, the Pan-Africa.
3: Key institutions
* Parliament of the African Union: is another important body of the African Union, with members as Heads of State or Heads of Government of the 55 Member States. This parliament is the most powerful supreme body in the African Union system because it is the voice of all its members. The National Assembly convenes once a year and decisions are made by a unanimous vote or by a two-thirds majority, as the case may be.
African Union Authority: An institution, which is the Secretariat of the African Union, composed of various missionaries and staff. The Secretariat is located in Addis Ababa, Ethiopia, and serves as the administrative body and coordinator of the general affairs of the Union and its members in meetings or archives.
* Executive Board: An institution that has a role after the African Union Parliament, as its composition is limited to the ministerial level of the member states. It has the role of deciding on issues such as foreign trade, social security, food, agriculture and responsible relations with the Union Parliament and preparing documents for the Union Parliament to make a final decision.
* Committee of Permanent Representatives: A special body composed of representatives from each member state, which is responsible for organizing the work for the Executive Board, also known as the Technical Body. It should be noted that there are currently 55 member countries of the African Union.
* The Security and Peace Council: is one of the top bodies of the African Union, which has the primary role of managing, overseeing and ensuring peace and security in the region as a whole, especially for Member States. The council was proposed in 2001 at the Union Summit in Lhasa and was formally established in 2004 by a protocol approved by the African Union Parliament in July 2002. .
* Technical committees: are other sub-bodies established under the Abuja Treaty, which allow them to work to facilitate and provide assistance to ministers to submit to the Union Parliament.
