Jean Malin: Politicians should not use International Human Rights Day to put pressure on independent courts to release defendants
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Chin Malin, Vice Chairman and Spokesman of the Cambodian Human Rights Committee, confirmed to Fresh News that politicians of all persuasions should not take December 10 International Human Rights Day as a political agenda to Put pressure on the courts, which are independent institutions, to demand the release of detainees unconstitutional.
"This day should not be used as a political agenda to criticize and demean the government and the authorities by making political messages that are provocative, exaggerated, distorted and contradictory," said Jean Malin. "Cambodia's constitution and laws, such as putting pressure on the courts, which are independent institutions, to release or drop charges against defendants who are in court proceedings."
In this regard, the spokesman also emphasized that the actions and actions to protect the law against those who violate the law are not to protect and promote human rights, but rather Has been encouraging people to more violate the law without fear and fear, and this can pose a serious risk to society.
To celebrate the 72nd anniversary of International Human Rights Day on December 10, Mr. Jean Malin urged all politicians to deliver a message and take action that is beneficial to society, especially messages that are meant to educate the people. Rights and laws and the exercise of legal rights and freedoms Avoid the exercise of rights that violate the rights and freedoms of others, public order and social security, which may eventually lead to legal challenges.
