General knowledge: The story behind the collapse of the former Soviet Union
During the Cold War, the Soviet Union faced many crises, both at home and abroad. Abroad, the Soviet Union released large sums of money to help some of the poorest countries in Asia, South America, and Africa for the sole purpose of expanding communist ideology.
Domestically, the Soviet government sought to expand agriculture, but over-applied chemical fertilizers and pesticides on soil and crops, causing serious environmental damage and damaging soil quality. Very bad for agricultural development. Corruption and child mortality are the main social crises.
Gobachev initially called for foreign investment in the industrial sector and encouraged competition in a free market economy. In politics, he allowed the opposition to openly criticize the socialist regime. He organized elections for party and government officials to show trust.
The Soviet Union amended its constitution on December 1, 1988, to hold multi-party elections. The new constitutional reform stipulates a five-year presidential term.
Political prisoners and dissidents have been released, while those deported to Siberia have been allowed to return to their homeland and their families.
After taking office as President of the Soviet Union, Gorbachev announced that he would no longer allow military intervention in Eastern Europe. It reminded the group of countries under the Soviet Union that they began to declare independence and shift to democracies like those in Western Europe, such as East Germany, Czechoslovakia, Hungary, Bulgaria and Poland.
The Soviet Red Army deployed in Eastern Europe was withdrawn from 1991-1994, while the Warsaw Pact was further shaken until its complete disappearance on June 28, 1991.
The formation of the Russian Federation ended Mikhail Gorbachev's presidency of the Soviet Union on December 25, 1991. Although supporters of the old socialist regime in the former Soviet Union were opposed to Gobachev's Peres Strike reforms, in the eyes of the people around the world, Gobachev was the savior of the world from the effects of the Cold War.
