The following is an announcement from the Ministry of Education: Life in Cambodia uses new technology to change the mindset of blaming victims
(Phnom Penh): Life in Cambodia is based in Siem Reap and is known as the winner of the annual award for the campaign to stop violence and gender harassment in Cambodia. Every year, Life in Cambodia organizes "16 Days to End Gender-Based Violence."
To make more Cambodians aware of this campaign, Life in Cambodia's 2020 Domestic Violence Campaign emphasizes not blaming the victims and the short film "Not Her Fault" Viewed almost 1.4 million times (as of December 7, 2020). Short films and related campaigns urge all Cambodians to turn to supporting women rather than blaming them.
In addition to sharing their stories, the women were also asked to attach photos of the clothes or describe the clothes they were wearing at the time.
"I think it's important to talk openly about this because it can show other victims that they are not alone, and that our society is lacking," she said. "And then we can start to change that gap."
Life in Cambodia brings together the stories of many women and selects 24 stories to represent different situations in the daily lives of women and girls. These stories are used to create an online exhibition that shows different times in different parts of a Cambodian house.
Asked about the use of technology in the exhibition, Billy Gorter, Executive Director of Life in Cambodia, said: "We hope that this exhibition will have more participants because it will allow us to spread the important message: "What a woman or a girl wears when she is the victim of violence, harassment or abuse has nothing to do with each other."
For the past 12 years, Life in Cambodia has been asking the Cambodian community about the most serious challenges they have faced, and domestic violence has always been one of the three main issues they raise. This statement comes as no surprise. One in three Cambodian men has admitted that they have used violence against their partners, which indicates the scale of the violence in Cambodia.
Of all women victims of violence, only 24% seek help, which is due to the fact that only 8% perceive violence as a crime.
According to the report before the incident, a large truck was seen driving down from the bridge to Kampong Cham city. People's houses as well.
