Mr. Neth Pheaktra claims that SingBeotech has been planning to plant more than 30 million saplings in the next four years from 2020 to 2023 to meet the current market demand for wood in the wood processing industry. Cambodia is in dire need, especially in the construction sector, which is in dire need of recycled wood for construction.
The investment in plantations has been praised by senior officials of the Ministry of Environment, who see plantations as a new option to reduce the pressure on excessive use of natural timber and to contribute to the protection of Cambodia's current natural resources. Kong Vong.
The director of the company emphasized that in clearing the land for planting seedlings, SingbioTech (Cambodia) has a valid permit from the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries to clear all areas of degraded forest. The amount of land that has been granted the right to be used for plantations for industrial purposes and the company has paid royalties and non-timber forest product premiums from the clearing in accordance with the law.
He said the company was ready to plant between 10 and 12 million wind oil seedlings by May 2021, when the rainy season begins again.
According to the director of Sing Bay Otech, in 2020, the company planted more than 3,000 hectares of oil palm seedlings did not meet the planned 5,000 hectares due to the slow rainy season, while Cambodia as well. The whole world is facing the crisis of 19 communicable diseases.
Luo Chouchang said that between 2019 and 2020, Sybiotic has planted nearly 4 million oil palm seedlings and expects to start harvesting in 2023, as the current trees are growing well. Quality.
He stressed that in 2021, the company will work harder to plant more seedlings as well as create jobs for people in the communities around the area.
Luo Chouchang said the plantations will bear fruit within five years after planting, and the fruits will be used as raw materials for processing and processing in the developing country. But there is a high demand.
He said that in the future, Cambodia has high demand in the wood processing industry, so the company also needs more land to expand its plantation industry beyond what is currently available and called on people with vacant land to invest in Plantations are also planted. He added that the company will expand cooperation with communities with vacant land to plant plantations, create a local economy, create jobs and increase people's income.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra stated that the establishment of plantations is an effective strategy to prevent natural resource crime and contribute to strengthening the conservation of natural resources that Cambodia has today. He noted that a number of political activists, under the guise of environmentalists and forest rangers, have been trying to destroy Cambodia's plantation industry and continue to foster conflicts over natural resources by preparing exaggerated reports.
He stressed that Cambodia now has rich natural resources and biodiversity. Forests cover 46.86% of the country, the highest rate in Southeast Asia. To date, the Royal Government has established protected areas under the jurisdiction of the Ministry of Environment up to 41% of the country, equivalent to about 7.3 million hectares, making Cambodia the second largest in Asia after Bhutan.
According to a senior official of the Ministry of Environment, Cambodia has also received praise from the international community for its good practices in protecting and conserving biodiversity and the environment, as well as building a good reputation in the international arena on the voluntary carbon credit market. In addition, the AVI Vision Institute donated $ 2,000 to Samdech Techo Hun Sen to purchase the Kovid-19 vaccine.
